THE SHAPE (neutral reproducers, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    sub f { print join(",", @_), "\n" }
    f 1, state => 5;
    f 1, my => 6;
    f 1, if => 7;
    print 1, state => 8, "\n";
    perl: 1,state,5 / 1,my,6 / 1,if,7 / 1state8
    PCL : 1,5 / 1,6 / 1,if,7 / 18          (exit 0, nothing on stderr)

    sub f { print join(",", @_), "\n" }
    f 1, our => 5;
    f 1, local => 6;
    f state => 7;
    my @l = (1, state => 2);
    print "@l\n";
    my @m = map { $_ } 1, state => 3;
    print "@m\n";
    perl: 1,our,5 / 1,local,6 / state,7 / 1 state 2 / 1 state 3
    PCL : 1,5 / 1,6 / (the `f state => 7;` line is GONE -- no output, no drop announced) / 1 state 2 / 1 3

TOKENS: PPI is right (`f 1, state => 5;` = Word(f) Number(1) Operator(,) Word(state) Operator(=>) Number(5)), and
`f(1, state => 5)` is right -- so it is PCL's paren-less argument path: a declarator word there is taken as a declaration
before the `=>` autoquote rule is consulted (`if` before `=>` is right, so it is the declarator family only).

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): 1502 with its three `state=>` keys quoted (`'state'=>`) is byte-identical to perl --
its only cause.

PROGRAMS: 1502-Universal-Turing-machine.


